## Custom Domain and DNS Configuration

After successful deployment, your app is available via a default (auto-generated) EasyPanel domain (e.g., `<app>.<project>.easypanel.host`). For production, you’ll almost always want a **custom domain**.

### Steps to Setup a Custom Domain

#### 1. Add DNS Records

- Log into your DNS provider (Cloudflare, Namecheap, AWS Route53, etc.).
- Create an **A record** pointing your domain/subdomain (`app.example.com`) to your EasyPanel server's public IP address.
- For wildcards or supporting subdomains, you may add a CNAME `*` record pointing to the domain.

**Example Table: Common DNS Settings**

| Record Type | Name / Subdomain  | Value (Target)                  | Purpose         |
|-------------|-------------------|---------------------------------|-----------------|
| A           | @ (root or blank) | <EasyPanel server IP>           | Root domain     |
| A           | app               | <EasyPanel server IP>           | Subdomain       |
| CNAME       | *                 | example.com                     | Wildcard        |

#### 2. Configure Domain in EasyPanel

- In your application service’s "Domains" tab, click "Add Domain".
- Enter your full custom domain (`app.example.com`) and set the port to your app’s internal port (default: 3000 for Next.js).
- Toggle **HTTPS** to enable SSL provisioning. Make the new domain "Primary" by clicking the star icon.
- Save changes. EasyPanel and Traefik will automatically request a Let’s Encrypt certificate and proxy traffic to your app.

**Wildcard Domain Support:** For SaaS/multitenant apps, refer to the Easypanel [wildcard domain guide] to configure Traefik with DNS challenge provider credentials.

#### 3. Remove the Default Domain (Optional)

- To avoid duplication, you can remove the Easypanel-generated domain after verifying your app works on the custom domain.

